example.paragraph:
Let $A = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      1 & 3 & 4 \\
      {- 4} & 2 & {- 6} \\
      {- 3} & {- 2} & {- 7}
      \end{bmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{b} = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      b_{1} \\
      b_{2} \\
      b_{3}
      \end{bmatrix}\,.$ Is the equation $A\mathbf{x} = \mathbf{b}$ consistent for all possible $b_{1}\text{,~}b_{2}\text{,~}b_{3}\text{?}$

Compute $A\mathbf{x}$, where $A\, = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      2 & 3 & 4 \\
      {- 1} & 5 & {- 3} \\
      6 & {- 2} & 8
      \end{bmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{x}\, = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      x_{1} \\
      x_{2} \\
      x_{3}
      \end{bmatrix}\,.$

paragraph.math:
$$\begin{matrix}
      {\begin{bmatrix}
      2 & 3 & 4 \\
      {- 1} & 5 & {- 3} \\
      6 & {- 2} & 8
      \end{bmatrix}\,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      x_{1} \\
      x_{2} \\
      x_{3}
      \end{bmatrix}} & {= x_{1}\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      {- 1} \\
      6
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\, + \,x_{2}\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      3 \\
      5 \\
      {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\, + \,x_{3}\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      4 \\
      {- 3} \\
      8
      \end{array} \right\rbrack} \\
       & {= \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {2x_{1}} \\
      {- x_{1}} \\
      {6x_{1}}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\, + \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {3x_{2}} \\
      {5x_{2}} \\
      {- 2x_{2}}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\, + \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {4x_{3}} \\
      {- 3x_{3}} \\
      {8x_{3}}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack} \\
       & {= \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      {2x_{1} + 3x_{2} + 4x_{3}} \\
      {- x_{1} + 5x_{2} - 3x_{3}} \\
      {6x_{1} - 2x_{2} + 8x_{3}}
      \end{bmatrix}}
      \end{matrix}$$

content.paragraph:
For simplicity, take $n = 3$, $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{lll}
      \textbf{a}_{1} & \textbf{a}_{2} & \textbf{a}_{3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, and $\mathbf{u}$, $\mathbf{v}$ in $\text{ℝ}^{3}$. (The proof of the general case is similar.) For $i = 1\text{,~}2\text{,~}3$, let $u_{i}$ and $v_{i}$ be the $i\text{th}$ entries in $\mathbf{u}$ and $\mathbf{v}$, respectively. To prove statement (a), compute $A(\mathbf{u} + \mathbf{v})$ as a linear combination of the columns of $A$ using the entries in $\mathbf{u} + \mathbf{v}$ as weights.

statement.paragraph:
Let $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      2 & {- 1} \\
      {- 6} & 3
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and $\mathbf{b} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      b_{1} \\
      b_{2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Show that the equation $A\mathbf{x} = \mathbf{b}$ does not have a solution for all possible $\mathbf{b}$, and describe the set of all $\mathbf{b}$ for which $A\mathbf{x} = \mathbf{b}$ does have a solution.

Repeat Exercise 15: $A = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      1 & {- 3} & {- 4} \\
      {- 3} & 2 & 6 \\
      5 & {- 1} & {- 8}
      \end{bmatrix}\,,$ $\mathbf{b} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      b_{1} \\
      b_{2} \\
      b_{3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,.$

Let $\mathbf{v}_{1} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      0 \\
      0 \\
      {- 2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,,$ $\mathbf{v}_{2} = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      0 \\
      {- 3} \\
      8
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,,$ $\mathbf{v}_{3} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      4 \\
      {- 1} \\
      {- 5}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,.$ ﻿

(T/F) The solution set of a linear system whose augmented matrix is $\lbrack\begin{array}{rrrr}
      \mathbf{a}_{1} & \mathbf{a}_{2} & \mathbf{a}_{3} & \mathbf{b}
      \end{array}\rbrack$ is the same as the solution set of $A\mathbf{x} = \mathbf{b}$, if $A = \lbrack\begin{array}{rrr}
      \mathbf{a}_{1} & \mathbf{a}_{2} & \mathbf{a}_{3}
      \end{array}\rbrack$.

(T/F) If the augmented matrix $\lbrack\begin{array}{rr}
      A & \mathbf{b}
      \end{array}\rbrack$ has a pivot position in every row, then the equation $A\mathbf{x} = \mathbf{b}$ is inconsistent.

Note that $\begin{bmatrix}
      4 & {- 3} & 1 \\
      5 & {- 2} & 5 \\
      {- 6} & 2 & {- 3}
      \end{bmatrix}\,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 3} \\
      {- 1} \\
      2
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\, = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 7} \\
      {- 3} \\
      10
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Use this fact (and no row operations) to find scalars $c_{1}$, $c_{2}$, $c_{3}$ such that $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 7} \\
      {- 3} \\
      10
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,\, = c_{1}\,{\,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      4 \\
      5 \\
      {- 6}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack}\,{\,{+ \,c_{2}}\,{\,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 3} \\
      {- 2} \\
      2
      \end{array} \right\rbrack}\,\,{+ \,c_{3}}\,{\,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      5 \\
      {- 3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,}.}$

It can be shown that $3\mathbf{u} - 5\mathbf{v} - \mathbf{w} = 0$. Use this fact (and no row operations) to find $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ that satisfy the equation $\begin{bmatrix}
      7 & 3 \\
      2 & 1 \\
      5 & 3
      \end{bmatrix}\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      x_{1} \\
      x_{2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\, = \,\,\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      6 \\
      1 \\
      0
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\,.$
